= Bessel function
{c}
{wiki}

= Bessel inverse-power expansion at infinity
{c}
{parent=Bessel function}
{title2=$w\sim e^{\pm iz}\sum_{k\geq0}c_kz^{-k}$}

= Bessel inverse-power expansions at infinity
{c}
{synonym}

For $w''+[1-A/z^2]w=0$, with $A=n^2-1/4$, substitution gives $s=\pm i$, exponent zero and $c_{k+1}=[k(k+1)-A]c_k/[2s(k+1)]$. Thus the first correction is $c_1=sAc_0/2$. For integer $n$ no coefficient truncation occurs and coefficient ratios grow as $k/(2s)$; this is an <asymptotic expansion>, not a convergent series at a finite $z$. The leading <Liouville–Green approximation> $q^{-1/4}e^{\pm i\int\sqrt q}$ has the same $1/z$ correction.

= Hankel function
{parent=Bessel function}
{c}
{title2=$H_\nu^{(1)},H_\nu^{(2)}$}

The two Hankel functions $H_\nu^{(1)}=J_\nu+iY_\nu$ and $H_\nu^{(2)}=J_\nu-iY_\nu$ solve the <Bessel differential equation>. Their large-argument forms distinguish outgoing and incoming oscillations and have sector-dependent <asymptotic expansions>.

= Hankel asymptotic sectors
{parent=Hankel function}
{c}

For fixed order, the standard <Hankel function> expansions in $e^{\pm iz}z^{-1/2}$ are valid in different sectors on a chosen argument sheet. Analytic continuation can mix exponentials; the sectors must be specified rather than treating a formal inverse-power series as global.

= Bessel function of the second kind
{parent=Bessel function}
{c}
{title2=$Y_\nu(z)$}

= Bessel functions of the second kind
{c}
{synonym}

For noninteger order, $Y_\nu=(\cos(\pi\nu)J_\nu-J_{-\nu})/\sin(\pi\nu)$; the integer-order function is defined by a limit. It gives a second independent solution of the <Bessel differential equation>. At order zero, $Y_0(z)\sim(2/\pi)[\log(z/2)+\gamma]$ near positive zero and $Y_0(z)\sim\sqrt{2/(\pi z)}\sin(z-\pi/4)$ at large positive argument, where $\gamma$ is the <Euler--Mascheroni constant>.

= Spherical Bessel function of the second kind
{parent=Bessel function of the second kind}
{title2=$n_\ell(z)$}

= Spherical Neumann function
{synonym}

The spherical second-kind function is $n_\ell(z)=\sqrt{\pi/(2z)}Y_{\ell+1/2}(z)$. Together with the first-kind <spherical Bessel function>, it spans the radial free-wave solutions; $n_\ell(z)\sim-\cos(z-\ell\pi/2)/z$ at large positive $z$.

= Spherical Bessel function
{title2=$j_\ell(x)=\sqrt{\pi/(2x)}J_{\ell+1/2}(x)$}
{c}
{parent=Bessel function}

The spherical Bessel functions solve the radial wave equation in three dimensions. In particular,
$$
j_2(x)=\left(\frac3{x^3}-\frac1x\right)\sin x-\frac3{x^2}\cos x,
\qquad j_2(x)=\frac{x^2}{15}+O(x^4).
$$
The apparent singularity at the origin is removable, as follows from the power series of the <Bessel function of the first kind>.

= Spherical Helmholtz matching to a decaying harmonic exterior
{parent=Spherical Bessel function}
{title2=$xj_\ell'(x)+(\ell+1)j_\ell(x)=xj_{\ell-1}(x)=0$}

A scalar field solving the <Helmholtz equation> inside a sphere of radius $R$ and <Laplace's equation> outside has a regular interior mode $b\,j_\ell(\sqrt{s}\,r)P_\ell(\cos\theta)$ and a decaying exterior mode $a r^{-\ell-1}P_\ell(\cos\theta)$. Continuity of the field and its radial derivative gives, for $x=\sqrt{s}R>0$,
$$
xj_\ell'(x)+(\ell+1)j_\ell(x)=0.
$$
The <spherical Bessel function> recurrence turns this into $j_{\ell-1}(x)=0$, not $j_\ell(x)=0$. This elimination does not require division by a possibly vanishing boundary value.

For $\ell=0$, $j_0(x)=\sin x/x$ and $j_{-1}(x)=\cos x/x$, so the first positive root is $\pi/2$. For $\ell=1$, the matching condition is $j_0(x)=0$, whose first positive root is $\pi$. The distinction matters when a scalar model permits the monopole sector but a physical multipole model excludes it.

To compare angular sectors, put $u=rj_\ell(\sqrt{s}r)$. Its equation and boundary conditions are $-u''+\ell(\ell+1)u/r^2=su$, $u(0)=0$ and $u'(R)+\ell u(R)/R=0$. Its <Rayleigh quotient> is
$$
s=\frac{\int_0^R[|u'|^2+\ell(\ell+1)|u|^2/r^2]dr+\ell|u(R)|^2/R}{\int_0^R|u|^2dr}.
$$
Both added terms are nonnegative and increase with $\ell$. Thus the lowest scalar eigenvalue is $\pi^2/(4R^2)$ in the $\ell=0$ sector; if only $\ell\geq1$ is admitted, the lowest is $\pi^2/R^2$ in the $\ell=1$ sector. Zeros at $x=0$ caused by the vanishing normalization of $j_\ell$ are not additional eigenvalues.

= Frobenius resonance in the spherical Bessel equation
{c}
{parent=Spherical Bessel function}
{title2=$\sigma=\ell,-\ell-1$}

For $x^2y''+2xy'+[x^2-\ell(\ell+1)]y=0$ with integer $\ell\geq0$, the <Frobenius method> gives $\sigma=\ell$ or $-\ell-1$. The general coefficient equation is $[(j+\sigma)(j+\sigma+1)-\ell(\ell+1)]a_j=-a_{j-2}$, taking negative-index coefficients as zero. The regular branch has $a_1=0$ and $a_j=-a_{j-2}/[j(j+2\ell+1)]$. The singular branch has $a_j=-a_{j-2}/[j(j-2\ell-1)]$ except at $j=2\ell+1$. That resonant equation is compatible because its earlier odd coefficient vanishes; the newly free coefficient adds a multiple of the regular solution. Setting it to zero gives a pure even series times $x^{-\ell-1}$, independent of the regular branch, with no logarithmic term. For $\ell=0$ the resonance occurs already at $j=1$.

The normalization $a_0=2^\ell\ell!/(2\ell+1)!$ on the regular branch gives the <spherical Bessel function>
$$
j_\ell(x)=2^\ell x^\ell\sum_{s=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^s(s+\ell)!}{s!(2s+2\ell+1)!}x^{2s}.
$$
In particular $j_0(x)=\sin x/x$, with removable value one at zero.

= Logarithmic spherical Bessel square integral
{parent=Spherical Bessel function}
{title2=$\int_0^\infty j_\ell(x)^2\,dx/x=1/[2\ell(\ell+1)]$}

For integer $\ell\geq1$, put $I_\ell=\int_0^\infty j_\ell(x)^2\,dx/x$ and $J_\ell=\int_0^\infty j_\ell(x)j_{\ell-1}(x)\,dx$. Integrating $j_\ell'=j_{\ell-1}-(\ell+1)j_\ell/x$ gives $J_\ell=(\ell+1)I_\ell$. For $\ell>1$, integrating $j_{\ell-1}'=(\ell-1)j_{\ell-1}/x-j_\ell$ also gives $J_\ell=(\ell-1)I_{\ell-1}$. For $\ell=1$, the boundary values $j_0(0)=1$ and $j_0(\infty)=0$ give $J_1=1/2$, hence $I_1=1/4$. The recurrence proves the displayed identity. The integral without the square is different; for example $\int_0^\infty j_1(x)\,dx/x=\pi/4$.

= Spherical Hankel function
{parent=Spherical Bessel function}
{title2=$h_n^{(1)}=j_n+iy_n,\quad h_n^{(2)}=j_n-iy_n$}

These combinations of <spherical Bessel functions> solve the radial <Helmholtz equation>. For time dependence $e^{-i\omega t}$, $h_n^{(1)}(kr)$ is outgoing and $h_n^{(2)}(kr)$ incoming. At fixed order and large argument, $h_n^{(1)}(t)\sim(-i)^{n+1}e^{it}/t$. Their recurrence is $h_{n+1}=(2n+1)h_n/t-h_{n-1}$.

= Fixed-argument growth of spherical Hankel functions
{parent=Spherical Hankel function}
{title2=$h_n^{(1)}(t)\sim-i\sqrt2\,t^{-1}(2n/(et))^n$}

For fixed $t>0$ and large integer order, $y_n(t)\sim-(2n-1)!!/t^{n+1}$ while $j_n(t)$ is negligible. Stirling's formula gives $(2n-1)!!=(2n)!/(2^nn!)\sim\sqrt2(2n/e)^n$, proving the displayed asymptotic. In particular its magnitude is bounded above and below by constant multiples of $(2n/(et))^n$. This two-sided comparison is stronger than a one-sided big-O estimate and is what converts a finite spherical trace norm into a weighted condition on far-field coefficients.

= Spherical Bessel product integral
{parent=Spherical Bessel function}
{title2=$\int_0^\infty j_\ell(kr)j_\ell(kR)\,dk=\frac{\pi}{2(2\ell+1)}\frac{r_<^\ell}{r_>^{\ell+1}}$}

For integer $\ell\geq0$ and $r,R>0$, $r_<=\min(r,R)$ and $r_>=\max(r,R)$. A proof compares the <multipole expansion> of $1/|\mathbf r-\mathbf R|$ with its <Fourier transform of inverse distance in three dimensions>. Applying the <Rayleigh plane-wave expansion> to the Fourier representation gives coefficient $8\int_0^\infty j_\ell(kr)j_\ell(kR)\,dk$ multiplying $Y_{\ell m}(\hat r)Y_{\ell m}^*(\hat R)$; the Coulomb multipole coefficient is $4\pi r_<^\ell/[(2\ell+1)r_>^{\ell+1}]$. Equating the coefficients proves the identity. The powers also match continuously at $r=R$.

= Bessel function of the first kind
{title2=$J_\nu(z)$}
{c}
{parent=Bessel function}

= Bessel functions of the first kind
{c}
{synonym}

The Bessel function of the first kind $J_\nu$ is the solution of the <Bessel differential equation> selected by its regular power-series behavior at the origin. For $\operatorname{Re}\nu>-1/2$, it has the Poisson integral representation
$$
J_\nu(z)=\frac{(z/2)^\nu}{\Gamma(\nu+1/2)\sqrt\pi}
\int_{-1}^{1}e^{izt}(1-t^2)^{\nu-1/2}\,dt.
$$

= Integer-order Bessel reflection identity
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}
{c}
{title2=$J_{-m}(z)=(-1)^mJ_m(z)$}

For integer $m\geq0$, $J_{-m}(z)=(-1)^mJ_m(z)$. Thus negative integer orders remain regular at zero. The familiar $J_m(z)\sim z^m/(2^mm!)$ formula is used for nonnegative order; a negative factorial is not a valid continuation.

= Bessel functions of order one half
{c}
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}
{title2=$J_{1/2}(x)=\sqrt{2/(\pi x)}\sin x,\quad J_{-1/2}(x)=\sqrt{2/(\pi x)}\cos x$}

For $x>0$, the <Bessel differential equation> of order $1/2$ has independent solutions $x^{-1/2}\sin x$ and $x^{-1/2}\cos x$. Substituting $y=x^{-1/2}u$ reduces it to $u''+u=0$. The normalization of the <Bessel function of the first kind> gives the displayed formulas for orders $1/2$ and $-1/2$. The <Frobenius method> obtains the same pair from exponents $1/2$ and $-1/2$: their even-coefficient series reduce to the sine and cosine <power series>. In the negative-exponent recurrence, the free coefficient at the next exponent can add the positive-exponent solution, an example of an <undetermined coefficient at Frobenius resonance>.

= Fourier transform of the Bessel function J0
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}
{c}

With the <Fourier transform> convention $\widehat f(\omega)=\int_{\mathbb R}f(t)e^{-i\omega t}\,dt$, the regular order-zero <Bessel function of the first kind> has the <tempered distribution> transform
$$
\boxed{\widehat{J_0}(\omega)=\frac{2\,\mathbf1_{(-1,1)}(\omega)}{\sqrt{1-\omega^2}}.}
$$
The density is <integrable> despite its endpoint singularities. <Fourier inversion> gives the equivalent <integral representation>
$$
J_0(t)=\frac1\pi\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{e^{it\omega}}{\sqrt{1-\omega^2}}\,d\omega=\frac1\pi\int_0^\pi\cos(t\cos\theta)\,d\theta.
$$
The second formula follows by $\omega=\cos\theta$ and cancellation of the odd imaginary part. It is the standard real integral representation of $J_0$.https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.9.E1[NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, equation 10.9.1]{ref}

Indeed, transforming the order-zero <Bessel differential equation> $t y''+y'+ty=0$ gives $(1-\omega^2)Y'-\omega Y=0$. Inside $(-1,1)$, its solutions are $C(1-\omega^2)^{-1/2}$. The compactly supported choice also solves the equation as a <distribution>: rewrite it as $[(1-\omega^2)Y]'+\omega Y=0$, and observe that $(1-\omega^2)Y=C\sqrt{1-\omega^2}\,\mathbf1_{(-1,1)}$ is continuous and zero at the two endpoints. Its <distributional derivative> has no endpoint <Dirac delta> terms. The inverse is regular at $t=0$, and $y(0)=1$ fixes $C=2$.

The transformed equation alone also allows separate constants on $\omega<-1$ and $\omega>1$. Selecting the compactly supported density amounts to selecting the solution regular at the origin, rather than every possible singular solution of the <Bessel differential equation>.

= Jacobi-Anger expansion
{c}
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}
{wiki=Jacobi–Anger_expansion}
{title2=$e^{\mathrm iz\sin\theta}=\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z}J_k(z)e^{\mathrm ik\theta}$}

A <Fourier series> expansion of a sinusoidally modulated complex <wave phase> in <Bessel functions of the first kind>.

= Bessel second-moment identity
{parent=Jacobi-Anger expansion}
{c}
{title2=$\sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}m^2J_m(z)^2=z^2/2$}

For real $z$, the <probabilities> $J_m(z)^2$ over integer angular-momentum modes satisfy $\sum_m m^2J_m(z)^2=z^2/2$. Differentiate the unit-modulus function $e^{iz\cos\theta}$ and use Parseval to obtain the identity; its zero first moment follows from the symmetry of opposite orders.

= Quartic truncation of the Bessel function J0
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}
{title2=$J_0(\rho)\approx(1-\rho^2/8)^2$}

The regular power series has $J_0(\rho)=1-\rho^2/4+\rho^4/64-\cdots$. Its quartic truncation has a double zero at $\rho=\sqrt8$, giving a rough disc-well energy estimate $4\hbar^2/(\mu a^2)$. A truncation zero is not an exact Bessel zero or a rigorous eigenvalue bound. The double-root behavior particularly warns against treating a low-order local expansion as accurate far from its center.

= Debye asymptotic for oscillatory Bessel functions
{c}
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}
{title2=$J_n(n\sec\alpha)\approx[2/(\pi n\tan\alpha)]^{1/2}\cos[n(\tan\alpha-\alpha)-\pi/4]$}

= Debye Bessel asymptotic
{c}
{synonym}

For fixed $0<\alpha<\pi/2$, the <Bessel function of the first kind> has an interior nondegenerate <stationary point> in its cosine integral. The <stationary phase method> gives the displayed oscillatory leading term and an additive $O(n^{-3/2})$ remainder. The approximation loses uniformity near $\alpha=0$, where the <stationary point> reaches the endpoint and the curvature vanishes. For negative integer-order arguments, parity supplies a factor $(-1)^n$.

= Large-argument asymptotic expansion of the Bessel function of the first kind
{parent=Bessel function of the first kind}

For fixed $\nu$, put
$$
\chi=z-\frac{\pi\nu}{2}-\frac\pi4,
\qquad
a_k(\nu)=\frac{\prod_{j=1}^k\left(4\nu^2-(2j-1)^2\right)}{k!8^k},
\qquad a_0=1.
$$
Then
$$
J_\nu(z)\sim\sqrt{\frac2{\pi z}}
\left[
\cos\chi\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^ma_{2m}(\nu)}{z^{2m}}
-\sin\chi\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^ma_{2m+1}(\nu)}{z^{2m+1}}
\right]
$$
as $|z|\to\infty$, uniformly for $|\arg z|\leq\pi-\delta$. In particular,
$$
J_\nu(z)\sim\sqrt{\frac2{\pi z}}
\left[
\cos\chi-\frac{4\nu^2-1}{8z}\sin\chi
-\frac{(4\nu^2-1)(4\nu^2-9)}{2!(8z)^2}\cos\chi+\cdots
\right].
$$

= Bessel differential equation
{c}
{parent=Bessel function}

= Bessel equation
{c}
{synonym}

The Bessel differential equation of order $\nu$ is
$$
x^2y''+xy'+(x^2-\nu^2)y=0.
$$
For noninteger $\nu$, $J_\nu$ and $J_{-\nu}$ form a linearly independent pair of solutions.

= Frobenius series for Bessel functions
{c}
{parent=Bessel differential equation}
{title2=$a_{2m}=-a_{2m-2}/[4m(m+\nu)]$}

For the <Bessel differential equation> $x^2y''+xy'+(x^2-\nu^2)y=0$, substitute $y=x^s\sum_{n\geq0}a_nx^n$. The <indicial equation> is $s^2-\nu^2=0$, and coefficients obey $[(n+s)^2-\nu^2]a_n=-a_{n-2}$. For $s=\nu\geq0$, $a_1=0$ and all odd coefficients vanish. Iteration gives $a_{2m}=(-1)^ma_0\Gamma(\nu+1)/[4^mm!\Gamma(m+\nu+1)]$. Choosing $a_0=1/[2^\nu\Gamma(\nu+1)]$ gives the <Bessel function of the first kind> $J_\nu$.

For noninteger $\nu$, choosing the even-power series with $s=-\nu$ replaces $m+\nu$ by $m-\nu$ and yields $J_{-\nu}$ with its standard normalization. These solutions are independent. For integer $\nu\geq0$, the first solution has coefficients $(-1)^m/[2^{2m+\nu}m!(m+\nu)!]$ and defines an entire function: the term ratio tends to zero at every finite complex argument. For noninteger order, the even analytic series converges everywhere, while the prefactor $x^{\pm\nu}$ requires a branch off the positive real axis.

= Sturm-Liouville formulation with Bessel order as eigenvalue
{parent=Bessel differential equation}
{c}
{title2=$(xy')'+\beta^2xy=n^2y/x$}

For fixed $\beta$, move the order term of the <Bessel equation> to the right: $x^2y''+xy'+\beta^2x^2y=n^2y$. The <integrating factor> for its leading differential expression is $1/x$, giving the displayed <Sturm-Liouville theory> form with coefficient $p=x$, potential $q=\beta^2x$, weight $w=1/x$ and <eigenvalue> $n^2$. The origin is a singular endpoint. The <integrating factor> must be normalized from a positive reference point; its defining logarithmic <integral> cannot literally start at zero. <Boundary conditions> are needed before this differential identity becomes a specified self-adjoint spectral problem.

= Modified Bessel function
{parent=Bessel function}

The modified Bessel equation of order zero is
$$
z^2y''+zy'-z^2y=0.
$$
Its solution regular at zero and normalized by $I_0(0)=1$ is the modified Bessel function $I_0$.

= Large-argument asymptotic expansion of a modified Bessel function
{parent=Modified Bessel function}

For fixed real $\nu$ as $z\to+\infty$, the <modified Bessel function of the first kind> and <modified Bessel function of the second kind> have <asymptotic expansions>
$$
I_\nu(z)\sim\frac{e^z}{\sqrt{2\pi z}}\sum_{j=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^ja_j(\nu)}{z^j},\qquad
K_\nu(z)\sim\sqrt{\frac\pi{2z}}e^{-z}\sum_{j=0}^\infty\frac{a_j(\nu)}{z^j},\qquad
a_0=1,\quad a_{j+1}=\frac{4\nu^2-(2j+1)^2}{8(j+1)}a_j.
$$
The expansion and its error bounds establish actual growing and decaying solutions, beyond formal matching of a <differential equation>. These formulas are documented in https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.40[NIST DLMF, equations 10.40.1–2].

= Modified Bessel function of the first kind
{c}
{parent=Modified Bessel function}
{title2=$I_\nu(z)$}

= Modified Bessel functions of the first kind
{c}
{synonym}

The solution of the <modified Bessel differential equation> with <power series> $I_\nu(z)=\sum_{j\geq0}(z/2)^{2j+\nu}/[j!\Gamma(j+\nu+1)]$, interpreted through the reciprocal <gamma function> at its zeros. For fixed real order it grows exponentially along the positive real axis.

= Modified Bessel differential equation
{c}
{parent=Modified Bessel function}
{title2=$z^2w''+zw'-(z^2+\nu^2)w=0$}

The <differential equation> defining <modified Bessel functions> of order $\nu$. For real fixed $\nu$ and positive $z$, its independent standard solutions are $I_\nu(z)$ and $K_\nu(z)$.

= Modified Bessel function of the second kind
{title2=$K_\nu$}
{c}
{parent=Modified Bessel function}

= Modified Bessel functions of the second kind
{synonym}

The modified Bessel function of the second kind is the solution $K_\nu(x)$ of the modified Bessel differential equation that decays exponentially as $x\to+\infty$.

= Fourier transform of the modified Bessel function K0
{c}
{parent=Modified Bessel function of the second kind}
{title2=$\widehat{K_0(|x|)}(k)=\pi/\sqrt{1+k^2}$}

The <integral representation of the modified Bessel function of the second kind> gives $K_0(x)=\int_0^\infty e^{-x\cosh t}\,dt$ for $x>0$. Taking the <Fourier transform> of the even continuation and setting $u=\sinh t$ gives $2\int_0^\infty(1+k^2+u^2)^{-1}du=\pi/\sqrt{1+k^2}$. The large-<wavenumber> terms $\pi/|k|-\pi/(2|k|^3)$ are also obtained from the local <logarithmic singularity> $-\log|x|-(x^2/4)\log|x|$ using the <Fourier transform of the absolute logarithm>.

= Integral representation of the modified Bessel function of the second kind
{parent=Modified Bessel function of the second kind}

For real $x>0$ and $\nu$, one useful representation is
$$
K_\nu(x)=\frac12\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-x\cosh t+\nu t}\,dt.
$$
It turns large-order asymptotics into a <Laplace integral> with a moving maximum.

= Large-order asymptotic of the modified Bessel function of the second kind
{parent=Modified Bessel function of the second kind}

For fixed $x>0$,
$$
K_\nu(x)\sim\left(\frac\pi{2\nu}\right)^{1/2}
\left(\frac{2\nu}{ex}\right)^\nu
$$
as $\nu\to+\infty$. Applying <Laplace's method> to the <integral representation of the modified Bessel function of the second kind> proves the formula.

= Laplace integral method for a differential equation
{parent=Modified Bessel function}
{c}

Laplace's integral method seeks $y(z)=\int_Ce^{zt}f(t)\,dt$. Substitution into the differential equation and integration by parts transfer multiplication by $z$ into differentiation with respect to $t$, producing a first-order equation for $f$ and endpoint conditions for $C$.

= Laplace integral solution of a singular third-order equation
{parent=Laplace integral method for a differential equation}

For $x>0$, substituting $y(x)=\int_\gamma e^{xt}f(t)\,dt$ into $xy^{(3)}+2y=0$ and integrating by parts gives
$$
[e^{xt}t^3f(t)]_{\partial\gamma}
+\int_\gamma e^{xt}\{2f-(t^3f)'\}\,dt=0.
$$
The amplitude equation $(t^3f)'=2f$ has solution $f(t)=Ct^{-3}e^{-1/t^2}$. On $\gamma=(-\infty,0)$ both endpoint terms vanish, and hence
$$
y(x)=C\int_{-\infty}^0e^{xt-t^{-2}}t^{-3}\,dt
=C_1\int_0^\infty u e^{-u^2-x/u}\,du.
$$

= Laguerre contour-integral amplitude
{parent=Laplace integral method for a differential equation}
{c}

For the Laguerre equation
$$
zy''+(1-z)y'+\lambda y=0,
$$
the ansatz $y=\int_\gamma e^{zt}f(t)\,dt$ gives
$$
f(t)=t^{-\lambda-1}(t-1)^\lambda.
$$
The contour must make the endpoint contribution
$$
\left[e^{zt}t^{-\lambda}(t-1)^{\lambda+1}\right]_{\partial\gamma}
$$
vanish.

= Finite Laguerre contour solution
{parent=Laguerre contour-integral amplitude}
{c}

A finite contour based at an integrable branch point, or a closed contour around an integer-order pole, gives a solution analytic at $z=0$. It is therefore a constant multiple of the unique Frobenius power-series solution there.

= Integer-parameter Laguerre contour residues
{parent=Finite Laguerre contour solution}
{c}

For $\lambda=N\geq0$, the amplitude $(t-1)^N/t^{N+1}$ has a pole at zero whose contour residue is a degree-$N$ Laguerre polynomial in $z$. For $\lambda=-m<0$, the amplitude $t^{m-1}/(t-1)^m$ has a pole at one whose residue is $e^z$ times a polynomial of degree $m-1$.

= Real integral representation of the modified Bessel function I0
{parent=Laplace integral method for a differential equation}

The normalized order-zero function has the representation
$$
I_0(z)=\frac1\pi\int_{-1}^1\frac{e^{zs}}{\sqrt{1-s^2}}\,ds.
$$
